<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Italian governorate of Montenegro</h1><p class="st-meta">1941 CE – 1944 CE · capital Cetinje</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Italian-occupied Montenegro in the Second World War, where a planned puppet kingdom was swept away by one of the first mass uprisings in occupied Europe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the conquest of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia</a> in April 1941, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> occupied <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/montenegro">Montenegro</a> and planned to restore it as a separate state under Italian protection. On 12 July 1941 an assembly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetinje">Cetinje</a> proclaimed the restoration of Montenegro's independence. The next day, 13 July, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-partisans">Communist-led Partisans</a> and other insurgents launched a general uprising that liberated most of the country.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Italian forces reconquered the towns, and in October 1941 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/montenegro">Montenegro</a> was placed under a military governor, General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-pirzio-biroli">Alessandro Pirzio Biroli</a>, with harsh reprisals. The occupiers cooperated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chetniks">Chetnik</a> and separatist groups against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-partisans">Partisans</a>, and much of the mountainous interior remained a battleground between them.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> capitulated in September 1943, German forces took over the occupation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-partisans">Partisans</a> liberated <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/montenegro">Montenegro</a> by December 1944, and it became a republic of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia</a>. The 13 July uprising is a Montenegrin national holiday.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/italian-governorate-of-montenegro.jpg" alt="Italian governorate of Montenegro" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: F l a n k e r · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Italy_(1861-1946)_crowned.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1941 CE – 1944 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cetinje</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Alessandro Pirzio Biroli</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Serbian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Serbian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/montenegro">Montenegro</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Governorate of Montenegro, Independent State of Montenegro (1941)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1942&amp;focus=italian-governorate-of-montenegro" aria-label="Italian governorate of Montenegro on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5139 670 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Italian governorate of Montenegro lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5459 926L5304 942M5459 926L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5459 926h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5459 926h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Cetinje. <a href="/atlas?year=1942&amp;focus=italian-governorate-of-montenegro">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

